A||||13||Gokart||stainless steel exhaust question||||5||
Z||000000||Gokart||01-29-2000||06:39 PM||||The mechanic I bought my parts car from told me that the guy who had it just put in a stainless steel exhaust. It probably sat for about 2 years but was still suprised when I took it off to find spot rust on it here and there. If it's stainless steel shouldn't there be none at all? Anyway, I sanded the rust spots off and now I'm wondering if stainless steel is just a coating that is put on the exhaust or is it a type of steel? I looked into exhaust high temp paint and found that it is very expensive. I've heard some people using high temp BBQ spray!? Would this last or burn off? There's nothing wrong with the exhaust system I have now, but it'll be nice having a backup ready to go when it does s**t the bed. TIA for any replys to my questions.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cooter||01-29-2000||07:12 PM||||A lot of cars have stainless pipe ahead of the converter, but it looks like it is rusted because of the crud that is stuck to it. Could this be what is on your pipes? Anyway, there are other high temp paints other than BBQ paint that should hold up on a muffler. I just painted a very large BBQ grill with high temp black exhaust paint and it looks great. The paint held up fine during the use of the grill, so I would not be afraid to use it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||01-29-2000||07:16 PM||||I don't know anything about BBQ sprays.  But I do know that SS will not rust.  It does sometimes corrode.  If what you sanded off was truly rust then it wasn't SS you were dealing with.  Maybe it was galvanized steel.  That has a look that sort of reminds me of raw SS except that most of the gal. I've seen has that particle board look.  I guess it's possible that someone had sprayed something on a regular steel exhaust to make it look like raw SS.  Once again, SS does not rust.  That's why most SS exhaust systems are warranteed forever (or at least a long time).||||reg||1
Z||000003||TOM||01-29-2000||08:40 PM||||Ok here we go with todays  alloy lesion. The term stainless steel covers many different grades. A stainless kitchen knife will rust. That is because there is carbon in it to make it tougher and to make it hold an edge. A real low carbon stainless will not rust but is also not very strong and wont keep an edge better than a plastic comb. If this does not help I will go deeper. The header pipes are stainless and trey rust and can be welded with mild rod which is recommended because of the amount of thermal cycles they have gone through ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||01-29-2000||08:46 PM||||Tom- Good point. Can a MIG welder be used to weld the manifolds? I have a set that are in need of repair, but I only have access to a wire welder.||||reg||1
Z||000005||TOM||01-29-2000||09:54 PM||||Yes that is what I use. I have never had one come back because of my weld failing. First get a 5 gallon bucket and a two gallon box of pool acid. Its under $10 mix the acid with water 1 to 1. soac the manifold in it for a coupe hours. This get them nice and clean for welding. Also you can get it hot then quench it in the acid it will clean it immediatley||||reg||1
Z||000006||GREENDANO||01-30-2000||08:44 AM||||Doesn't quenching the weld in water make the piece brittle?  I was always taught to let the weld cool on it's own.  I can't imagine the stink that manifold makes quenching it in the acid mix.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <BR>Here's another tip:  Bolt the manifold to an old head, if you can, before welding it.  That'll keep warpage almost to nothing.  I bolted my new ones up and fixed the inner slag intrusions before they ever saw exhaust.  I don't have the number nearby, but there's a company in PA that will computer bend stainless tubes for yor exhaust.  You either send them the old ones for a pattern, make a cobbled exhaust as a pattern or use convoluted tubing bent to the shape you want, fill the tubes with expanding foam and cut it apart for shipping.  They have a special  scanner measure the tubes and a computerized bender spits out your tubes.  The cost between mild and stainless was only a couple bucks...  $3.50/ft mild  around $5 for stainless.<BR>  Dan-O||||reg||1
Z||000007||squid||01-30-2000||09:02 AM||||GreenDano, whats the name, phone, and  address of that company? Is it 304 stainless? Do they use a mandrel bender that wont reduce the inside diameter on sharp bends?||||reg||1
Z||000008||GREENDANO||01-30-2000||06:07 PM||||Here's the number:1-800-878-3635<BR>Stainless Works is the company's name.<BR>Yes, they use a computerized mandrel bender.<BR>  Dan-O||||reg||1
Z||000009||Jim Baumann||01-30-2000||06:39 PM||||Is getting your own set of pipes bent better for performance or is it better to go with a set of those Borlas that are being offered in one of yesterday's threads?<p>[This message has been edited by Jim Baumann (edited 01-30-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000010||mwbackus||01-30-2000||11:02 PM||||Street Legal Performance offers their 304 stainless steel Power-Flo mufflers for $210.  This is the same design as the Flowmaster #42583.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.slponline.com/slpperformanceparts/page_16.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.slponline.com/slpperformanceparts/page_16.html</A> <P>With the rest of the cat. back in stainless, one would have a pretty nice exhaust.  Any idea as to what Stainless Work's price for the pipes needed to complete a cat. back system like this would be?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||fierosound||01-31-2000||12:07 PM||||I think $420 is not a bad price for the BORLA system at all!   I've looked through the Fiero Store catalog - stock cat back systems are $180, Ocelot aluminized pipe cat backs are $270.  If you compare the price of Borlas on other cars - prices range from $400 to over $1000!!!!  As I plan to upgrade to a 3.4L with headers, I think the BORLA exhaust will "complete" the exhaust system very nicely...||||reg||10
Z||000012||Monkeyman||01-31-2000||04:48 PM||||When I get done with my '85 GT, I'm gonna cut the top and bottoms off of a whole bunch of soup cans and duct tape them together for my exhaust.  It  may be loud (and leak a little) but I'll bet it's cheaper than everyone elses! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||GREENDANO||01-31-2000||06:36 PM||||Actually the company does most of their business with street rodders and custom cars or maybe a Fiero with a V8, like mine.  Performance between mild and stainless steel would be about the same.  The difference is the stainless will last longer.<BR>  Dan-O||||reg||1
